.Dd August 9, 2026
.Dt JEEP-META 1
.Os Jeep DEVEL
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm jeep-meta
.Nd edit top-level metadata in a Janet bundle
.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar key
.Ar value
.No ...
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
edits top-level key-value pairs in a bundle’s
.Sy info file .
.Pp
Each
.Ar key
is set to the
.Ar value
that follows it.
A
.Ar key
that is already present has its
.Ar value
replaced and a
.Ar key
that is absent is added.
Keys that are not named are left untouched and a
.Ar value
of
.Ql "nil"
removes the
.Ar key .
If a named
.Ar key
is absent and its
.Ar value
is
.Ql "nil" ,
the pair is skipped.
.Pp
The
.Ql ":name"
key must be a string and cannot be removed,
because an
.Sy info file
without a string name is not valid.
It can still be given a new value.
No pair is applied if any pair in the same invocation breaks
either rule.
.
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ar key
.No ...
.Xc
One or more keys to set.
Each key must be a keyword (e.g.
.Ql ":author" ) .
.It Xo
.Ar value
.No ...
.Xc
The value for each key.
How a value is read is described in
.Sx "Values"
below.
.El
.
.Ss Values
A
.Ar value
is read as a JDN value only if it reads whole and is not a symbol.
Anything else is taken as a string.
In practice this means the following are read as JDN:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -offset 3n -compact
.It
numbers,
such as
.Ql "42" ,
.Ql "-1"
and
.Ql "3\&.14" ;
.It
the booleans
.Ql "true"
and
.Ql "false" ;
.It
.Ql "nil" ,
which removes the key;
.It
keywords,
such as
.Ql ":library" ;
.It
collections,
such as
.Ql "\(lB:dev\(rB"
and
.Ql "\(lB\(dqa\&.janet\(dq \(dqLICENSE\(dq\(rB" ;
and
.It
quoted strings,
such as
.Ql "\(dqa string\(dq" .
.El
.Pp
Everything else is a string.
That covers bare words such as
.Ql "MIT" ,
.Eo
.Pa https://example.org/foo
.Ec
and
.Eo
.Pa deps/foo
.Ec ,
which Janet would otherwise read as symbols,
as well as phrases such as
.Ql "A Programmer"
and anything that does not read at all,
such as
.Ql "1\&.0\&.0"
and
.Ql "2026-08-09" .
The common case therefore needs no quoting beyond what the shell
requires.
.Pp
A value that should be a string despite looking like a number
or a boolean has to be quoted.
This matters most for tags and versions that are made up only
of digits,
because
.Ql "20240101"
is read as a number and a leading zero would be lost.
Such a value is written as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ jeep meta :version \(aq\(dq20240101\(dq\(aq
.Ed
.Pp
The same rules apply to the values given to
.Xr jeep-dep 1 .
.Pp
For more information about the
.Sy info file ,
see CONCEPTS in
.Xr jeep 1 .
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
To add metadata to the
.Sy info file :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ jeep meta :author \(dqA Programmer\(dq :license \(dqMIT\(dq
.Ed
.Pp
To change existing metadata:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ jeep meta :author \(dqA Programmer <a\&.programmer@example\&.org>\(dq
.Ed
.Pp
To remove metadata from the
.Sy info file :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ jeep meta :author nil
.Ed
.Pp
To set metadata to a value that is not a string:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ jeep meta :private true
.Ed
.
